 that he takes up on her mind? How she watches him like she watches no othe=
r, listens like she hears no other. . . with habit, she had come to learn h=
is rhythms and the curves of his mind. To know him and watch over him came =
as naturally to her as does to a mother for her child. With every new event=
 that she perceived- the slightest swing in his gait, the gentlest bounce t=
o his tone- she found the purest of joys reflected within her. It was a cel=
estial connection, she said, one that no distance or time could sever.

She was a blip on his horizon. An interesting phenomenon, he thought, for s=
omething about her was off. He couldn=E2=80=99t quite place a finger on it.=
 Was it how she never seemed to blink? Or how she always seemed to live an =
inch below your skin? A subliminal existence, most intriguing=E2=80=A6 and =
in a moment, the interesting phenomenon is brushed off his plane of conscio=
usness- he had things to attend to, a life to live, a lover to caress.

Anon, the interesting phenomenon appears again, and he casts a curious eye =
in her direction. Sometimes, she elicits his chuckle, maybe a question or a=
 remark in passing, then he returns to his day. She, however, will preserve=
 and feed off this morsel that he has thrown her way for yet another foreve=
r, until she is visible again, a blip on his horizon. He sees her- almost, =
but not quite- subliminal at best.
