 133.333%;"><img alt=3D"Down came a blackbird
And pecked off her nose.
She wasn't even in the garden,
Hanging out clothes.

If there's one thing I've learnt in my twenty years of existence, it is tha=
t you don't take a rhyme at face value. (Agatha Christie nods in approval)=
=20
Apparently, songbirds baked alive in a pie, so that they could fly out and =
sing when the pie was cut open, was an actual delicacy in 16th century Ital=
y.=20
And the entire rhyme may be symbolic of events in the English Reformation. =
The bread-and-honey-eating queen was the first wife of some self-important =
king and the maid was his second. She was later hanged for treason. The bla=
ckbirds taking her nose is symbolic of the demon stealing her soul.

Pretty cheery for a kid's story heh? Wait till you hear all about The Pied =
Piper. And Ring Ringa Roses. (Source: Wikipedia)

PS: If you don't know what I'm talking about, go look up the nursery rhyme =
'Sing a song of sixpence', you knucklehead. Fogetting nursery rhymes, it se=
ems. I hope a blackbird bites your nose off.

